fn default_phases() -> Vec<WorkflowPhase> {
    vec![
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s0_admission",
            stage_id: "stage0",
            stage_code: "S0",
            title: "S0 admission and project index",
            gate: PhaseGate::S0Admission,
            hard_gate: false,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S0 Admission and one SERVICE_STATUS% marker. Verify source path, local target queue, service reachability, app scale, framework/tech hints, route count hints, and whether the target appears intentionally vulnerable or organically developed. Do not report vulnerabilities. S0 is not complete until SERVICE_STATUS% reachable, SERVICE_STATUS% started, or SERVICE_STATUS% blocked is emitted.",
            body: "Check the source tree and local or user-provided target with lightweight commands. Build a compact project index: language/framework, server entrypoint, route directories, frontend directory, config files, data stores, upload/file surfaces, auth/session primitives, and likely test credentials if source-visible. Emit BREADTH% with an initial scale guess. If the service is down and the objective authorized local starts, recover it in S0 rather than deferring to S4: start it with a durable background launcher, capture logs, poll the health URL until you get an actual HTTP response, and only then emit SERVICE_STATUS% started. If startup fails, inspect the startup log and emit SERVICE_STATUS% blocked with the reason instead of pretending the target is alive.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s1_route_surface",
            stage_id: "stage1",
            stage_code: "S1",
            title: "S1 feature fingerprint and route/API boundary map",
            gate: PhaseGate::S1Surface,
            hard_gate: false,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S1 Recon plus a compact SURFACE%/OBLIGATION%/READ_TARGET% ledger for high-risk route, auth, object-id, middleware, schema/docs, and source/sink boundaries. This phase is invalid if it only says it will emit a ledger.",
            body: "Map network-reachable endpoints and route registration fast. Prefer rg, router tables, entrypoints, SCANNER_CONTEXT, and file lists over broad cat of every handler. S1 is a security index for S2, not a generic route map: cover these five cheap global indexes before stopping: route_api_index, auth_session_jwt_index, object_ownership_index, source_sink_index, and config_docs_debug_index. Convert high-value SCANNER_FACT% lines into existing READ_TARGET% or OBLIGATION% rows; do not invent scanner-specific output markers. Keep prose minimal: do not narrate routine planning with phrases like \"Let me\", \"I need\", or \"Now I\". Do not use marker names as Markdown headings; marker lines must be bare machine-readable lines. Marker field hygiene is mandatory: category=<single-taxonomy-token> only; put file/route in target/source/path, sink in sink, and short obligation name in debt. Never pack debt/source/sink/reason into category or target. Cover HTTP method/path, public/protected/admin hints, object identifiers, middleware, major request fields, API schemas/docs, helper utilities, support/dev routes, frontend routes that call backend APIs, and API paths mined from OpenAPI/Swagger/GraphQL/JavaScript bundles present in the source tree. Keep generic high-risk web families visible: auth bypass, session/JWT/key trust, object ownership/IDOR, account recovery/change flows, MFA/security-question/CAPTCHA anti-automation, file/key/log exposure, SSRF/redirect, rendered browser sinks, pricing/entitlement/order/review/export workflow abuse, and metrics/docs/config/secrets. SURFACE_BUDGET% stage=s1 total_max=90 route_phase_max=45 per_category_max=6 role=map_not_task. Emit SURFACE% only for representative backend state; use COVERAGE% for counts and inventories. READ_TARGET_BUDGET% stage=s1 total_max=25 route_phase_max=10 per_lane_max=5 priority=high_only. In this route/API phase, emit at most 10 READ_TARGET% lines total, no more than 5 for any lane, and do not emit medium/low priority read targets. A READ_TARGET% is an S2 deep-read work order, not a vulnerability conclusion or a checklist of every interesting file: lane=<identity_engine|injection_engine|ingress_engine|logic_engine|config_engine|any> priority=high category=<domain> kind=<endpoint-control|sink-trace|parser-boundary|business-invariant|config-baseline> path=<file-or-route> anchor=<function-or-symbol> reason=<why-this-file-is-first> question=<source-to-sink-or-invariant-to-prove> stop_when=<fact-that-closes-or-delegates> broaden_after=<named-gap-that-justifies-extra-rg> follow=<one-helper-or-model>. For OBLIGATION%, prefer id=<stable-id> category=<single-taxonomy-token> target=<route-or-file> debt=<short-name> source=<file:line> sink=<symbol-or-helper> must=<one-check>. Lane ownership: identity=auth/session/object ownership/IDOR; injection=SQL/NoSQL/template/eval/browser sinks; ingress=upload/parser/file/traversal/SSRF/redirect; logic=money/order/coupon/quota/workflow state transitions; config=secrets/crypto/config/docs/dependency exposure. Choose one primary lane for each READ_TARGET%; record adjacent concerns as OBLIGATION% instead of duplicate READ_TARGET% lines. Deduplicate by path+kind+category; if multiple routes share one file, emit one READ_TARGET% and mention representative endpoints in reason. For IDOR/authz, prefer READ_TARGET% lane=identity_engine kind=endpoint-control with endpoint, object_id, auth_hint, and ownership_hint=unknown when ownership is not obvious. If a topic is useful but below the READ_TARGET% budget, emit OBLIGATION% debt instead of another READ_TARGET%. If deeper semantics require many file reads or live auth state, emit OBLIGATION% debt for S2 instead of doing the full audit in S1.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s1_source_sink",
            stage_id: "stage1",
            stage_code: "S1",
            title: "S1 source/sink/parser/egress and obligation map",
            gate: PhaseGate::S1Surface,
            hard_gate: false,
            contract: "Emit additional FEATURE%/SURFACE%/OBLIGATION%/READ_TARGET% and COVERAGE% markers for source-sink inventory. Include sinks even before deciding they are vulnerable. Do not create final findings.",
            body: "Sample the highest-risk source/sink families rather than exhaustively tracing every handler: raw SQL/NoSQL, eval/template/browser render, deserialization/XML/YAML, file read/write/path joins, zip extraction, upload handlers, outbound fetch/http clients, redirects, crypto/key handling, cookie/session/JWT code, logs/metrics/debug/docs/static directories, auth/account metadata endpoints, header/media/browser sinks, business workflow helpers, and helper-side policy checks such as middleware attachment, ownership checks, parser validators, or support routes. Use SCANNER_CONTEXT as a cheap candidate index and convert useful SCANNER_FACT% rows into existing OBLIGATION% or READ_TARGET% rows. Re-check the five S2-feeding indexes before ending S1: route_api_index, auth_session_jwt_index, object_ownership_index, source_sink_index, and config_docs_debug_index. Keep prose minimal: do not narrate routine planning with phrases like \"Let me\", \"I need\", or \"Now I\". Do not use marker names as Markdown headings; marker lines must be bare machine-readable lines. Marker field hygiene is mandatory: category=<single-taxonomy-token> only; put file/route in target/source/path, sink in sink, and short obligation name in debt. Never pack debt/source/sink/reason into category or target. Convert this map into explicit OBLIGATION% markers grounded in generic CVE-prior families: authorization gaps, injection, parser abuse, traversal, SSRF, secrets exposure, weak crypto, and workflow abuse. Keep generic high-risk web families visible for S2 even when S1 does not prove them: auth bypass, session/JWT/key trust, object ownership/IDOR, account recovery/change flows, MFA/security-question/CAPTCHA anti-automation, file/key/log exposure, SSRF/redirect, rendered browser sinks, pricing/entitlement/order/review/export workflow abuse, and metrics/docs/config/secrets. SURFACE_BUDGET% stage=s1 total_max=90 source_sink_additional_max=45 per_category_max=6 role=map_not_task. READ_TARGET_BUDGET% stage=s1 total_max=25 source_sink_additional_max=15 per_lane_max=5 priority=high_only. Emit only additional high-priority READ_TARGET% lines that were not already emitted in the route/API S1 phase; deduplicate by path+kind+category and keep one representative anchor per vulnerability family. Promote a sink to READ_TARGET% only when it gives S2 a precise question, stop condition, and one next file to follow; otherwise emit OBLIGATION% debt. For OBLIGATION%, prefer id=<stable-id> category=<single-taxonomy-token> target=<route-or-file> debt=<short-name> source=<file:line> sink=<symbol-or-helper> must=<one-check>. Do not turn every sink into a READ_TARGET%; if a lane already has 5 high-priority read targets, route the remaining items to OBLIGATION% debt. Bias logic_engine READ_TARGET% toward state transitions and business invariants, not generic endpoint lists. S1 must not prove the vulnerability; it only schedules the next deep read. When sink depth is obvious but time-consuming, mark the obligation and hand the deep trace to S2 instead of finishing it in S1.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s2_parallel_semantic_audit",
            stage_id: "stage2",
            stage_code: "S2",
            title: "S2 concurrent 6-lane candidate discovery",
            gate: PhaseGate::S2Lane,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S2 Audit: launching workflow-owned candidate discovery. The backend scheduler owns this phase: it starts five core child threads with bounded provider-friendly concurrency, records queued/running/retrying/done lane status, joins their CLAIM% ledgers, then starts one optional residual quick_hits_engine using the core claim output. The five core lanes are the hard gate; quick_hits_engine is a recall-recovery lane and must not block S3 if empty or failed. S2 emits candidate CLAIM% rows only; evidence, rejection, controls, verification, and final findings belong to S4/S5.",
            body: "The backend scheduler launches these core native child engines first: identity_engine, injection_engine, ingress_engine, logic_engine, and config_engine. Do not serialize the core engines in the root and do not do all five audits yourself. After the core lanes join, quick_hits_engine runs as a residual rg-first recovery sweep mapped back into the same taxonomy and should only emit missed high-yield CLAIM% rows. Child engines receive compact S1 READ_TARGET%, SOURCE_PACKET%, and SCANNER_PACKET% inputs plus a small cross-lane weak-signal obligation packet. Each child engine reads assigned targets, performs source-only micro-verification, and emits only concise CLAIM% candidates with next=poc:<stable-unit>. After the scheduler joins all S2 lanes, S3 consumes the claim pool plus unresolved high-value obligation debt for merge, clustering, and S4 verification planning.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s3_merge_foa",
            stage_id: "stage3",
            stage_code: "S3",
            title: "S3 merge, FoA, and debt ledger",
            gate: PhaseGate::S3Merge,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S3 Summary before any S4 marker. S3 is an output-only claim canonicalization step: consume the S2 CLAIM% pool plus unresolved high-value OBLIGATION% debt, preserve one-vulnerability-one-PoC granularity, and hand S4 a concise verification plan. Do not call tools, run commands, inspect source, probe the service, or emit final PoCs.",
            body: "Use MERGE_PACKET% mode=s3_claim_pool and CLAIM% rows in RUNBOOK_CONTEXT as the S3 input; some rows may be unresolved obligation debt with status=debt. Output bare marker rows only: RUNBOOK%, CLAIM%, MERGE%, optional CHAIN_CANDIDATE%, and BREADTH%. Do not emit DUPLICATE%, CANDIDATE%, PROBE%, CONTROL%, VERIFY%, REJECTED%, FINDING%, markdown tables, code fences, or routine planning prose. Reuse input claim ids exactly; do not invent CANON-*, CAND-*, CLM-*, <source>, unlinked-*, or placeholder ids. For each surviving PoC unit, choose one primary input id and re-emit exactly one CLAIM% id=<primary-input-id> category=<taxonomy> target=<route-or-file> status=<copied|seed|debt> level=<copied-or-signal> severity=<copied-or-unknown> title=<short> reason=<source-root-cause-or-obligation-debt> impact=<short> next=poc:<stable-poc-unit>. Convert unresolved obligation debt into CLAIM% status=debt when it still has a concrete target, source/sink, or security boundary worth S4 verification; merge it only when it is the same PoC unit as an existing claim. Emit MERGE% id=<duplicate-input-id> merge_into=<primary-input-id> reason=<same-poc-unit> only when the duplicate has a different source id and the same exploit action, same route or sink, same object boundary, and same payload primitive. Different next=poc units must stay separate even when they share a broad root cause: keep JWT none, JWT HS256 confusion, hardcoded private key, coupon abuse, negative-order totals, entitlement bypass, distinct object ownership bugs, distinct secrets, and distinct exploit primitives as separate S4 handoff claims. Emit CHAIN_CANDIDATE% only when two surviving claim families compose through a realistic trust boundary. Emit BREADTH% surfaces=<n> domains=<n> hypotheses=<n> scale=<tiny|small|medium|large|seeded_or_intentionally_vulnerable> note=<why>. If no merge is safe, still re-emit the surviving CLAIM% rows with original ids.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s4_auth_authz_session_controls",
            stage_id: "stage4",
            stage_code: "S4",
            title: "S4 targeted controls for auth/authz/session",
            gate: PhaseGate::S4Probe,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S4 Fuzz. Consume only MERGE_PACKET% mode=s4_handoff plus CLAIM% rows. Blanket skip is forbidden. For every in-scope handoff claim, emit PROBE% and CONTROL%, then exactly one VERIFY%, REJECTED%, or S4_SKIP%. Prose-only closure is invalid.",
            body: "Run targeted live probes and negative controls for auth bypass, password reset/change, security question, JWT/session/cookie, role/mass assignment, IDOR/BOLA, object ownership, admin gates, anti-automation, and rate limits. VERIFY% requires a positive runtime signal plus a same-condition negative control; otherwise use S4_SKIP% for blocked/unsafe/source-backed-only cases or REJECTED% when runtime behavior contradicts the claim. Handoff claims with status=debt still require explicit closure as VERIFY%, S4_SKIP%, or REJECTED%; do not silently drop them. PROBE% or VERIFY% must include replay=<single-line-curl-or-steps> or an equivalent concrete HTTP/CLI step that S5 can reuse. Mutating probes must use disposable users/objects where possible and CONTROL% must mention cleanup=<read-only|restored|disposable|not-restored:reason>. Do not create new late claims in this core phase. First useful output must be a tool call or PROBE%/CONTROL%/VERIFY%/REJECTED%/S4_SKIP%; no routine planning narration.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s4_injection_xss_controls",
            stage_id: "stage4",
            stage_code: "S4",
            title: "S4 targeted controls for injection/xss/eval",
            gate: PhaseGate::S4Probe,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S4 Fuzz and close every in-scope handoff claim with PROBE%, CONTROL%, then VERIFY%, REJECTED%, or S4_SKIP%. Do not call JSON reflection browser XSS without a render sink. Do not call timeout/503 full RCE without command execution proof.",
            body: "For SQL/NoSQL/template/command/eval/vm/XSS/CORS claims, run precise payload variants and negative controls: normal input vs injected predicate, escaped payload vs raw payload, rendered browser sink vs JSON-only echo, sanitizer-enabled vs bypass candidate, and code execution side effect vs DoS-only timeout. VERIFY% means S5 can draft a concrete PoC from the evidence; weak source-backed or partial exploitability becomes S4_SKIP%, and contradicted paths become REJECTED%. Handoff claims with status=debt still require explicit closure as VERIFY%, S4_SKIP%, or REJECTED%; do not silently drop them. PROBE% or VERIFY% must include replay=<single-line-curl-or-steps> or an equivalent concrete HTTP/CLI step that S5 can reuse. For CHAIN_CANDIDATE% entries, emit CHAIN_VERIFY% only after an isolated, reversible verification plan with explicit prerequisites. Mutating probes need cleanup in CONTROL%. Do not emit new late claims in this core phase; no routine planning narration.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s4_files_ssrf_business_controls",
            stage_id: "stage4",
            stage_code: "S4",
            title: "S4 targeted controls for files/SSRF/business/debug",
            gate: PhaseGate::S4Probe,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S4 Fuzz and close every in-scope handoff claim. Blanket 'source available so skip fuzz' is invalid; source-only or unsafe paths require per-claim S4_SKIP% with evidence.",
            body: "Probe upload/parser/file/traversal/SSRF/redirect/business/secrets/debug/crypto claims. SSRF needs observable server-side fetch behavior or downgrade. File claims need path resolution/write/read evidence and blocked-path controls. Business logic needs legal workflow vs bypass workflow. Secrets/config exposure needs public reachability and impact, not just source presence. Handoff claims with status=debt still require explicit closure as VERIFY%, S4_SKIP%, or REJECTED%; do not silently drop them. PROBE% or VERIFY% must include replay=<single-line-curl-or-steps> or an equivalent concrete HTTP/CLI step that S5 can reuse. Mutating probes must be read-only, disposable, restored, or explicitly marked not-restored in CONTROL%; if rollback is unsafe, emit S4_SKIP% instead of dirtying shared state. Use REJECTED% for blocked, overclaimed, or contradicted probes. Do not emit new late claims in this core phase; no routine planning narration.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s4_regression_sweep",
            stage_id: "stage4",
            stage_code: "S4",
            title: "S4 regression sweep against broad family corpus",
            gate: PhaseGate::S4Probe,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S4 Fuzz. This is a late recovery sweep only. Do not re-probe represented families. For any genuinely missing high-value family, emit CLAIM% origin=late-blackbox plus PROBE%/CONTROL%, then VERIFY%, REJECTED%, or S4_SKIP%.",
            body: "Compare MERGE_PACKET% mode=s4_handoff against the broad family corpus in GLOBAL_CONTRACT. Look only for high-value families not represented in the handoff: mass assignment, weak crypto/hash, hardcoded credentials/keys, exposed API/account-metadata records, 2FA secret storage, CAPTCHA/security-answer leakage, token/progress-code helpers, pricing/payment/order/review/feedback/entitlement business abuse, upload MIME/null-byte/extension bypass, public docs/metrics/logs/config/files, robots/static manifests, Swagger/OpenAPI exposure, parser abuse, archive traversal, open redirect, SSRF, header/host/media/browser sinks, and CORS/header/rate limit issues. Late claims must include origin=late-blackbox and evidence strength in reason=<...>. Do not let low-severity informational leaks replace real PoC families. Controls, cleanup discipline, and REJECTED%/S4_SKIP% rules still apply; no routine planning narration.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s5_adjudication_report",
            stage_id: "stage5",
            stage_code: "S5",
            title: "S5 final PoC bundle",
            gate: PhaseGate::S5Adjudicate,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "Emit RUNBOOK% S5 Verify and one canonical POC% entry for each surviving vulnerability family after dedupe. Use ADJUDICATE% only for material merge/drop/downgrade decisions. S5 packages PoCs; it does not rediscover vulnerabilities or run probes.",
            body: "Perform final quality control without sacrificing recall. Merge only same root cause + same route/sink + same exploit primitive. Drop only claims that are duplicates, contradicted by controls, out of scope, placeholders, or clearly non-security observations. If a claim has a source/root-cause anchor and plausible exploit impact but lacks live proof, keep it as generated-not-verified or needs-poc instead of deleting it. Do not publish blocked/rejected/weak_signal assessments, scanner-only rows, or missing-version/behavior leads as vulnerabilities. For every surviving family, emit exactly one POC% id=<POC-nnn> finding=<claim-or-finding-id> severity=<critical|high|medium|low> category=<taxonomy> code_path=<file-or-route> target=<endpoint-or-component> precondition=<auth/state/setup-or-none> replay=<single-line-curl-or-steps> expected=<observable-signal> impact=<short> verification=<verified|generated-not-verified|needs-poc> cleanup=<read-only|restored|disposable|none|not-restored:reason> evidence_refs=<ids>. Quote every field value that contains spaces, shell quotes, JSON, semicolons, arrows, or angle brackets; especially target/precondition/replay/expected/impact/evidence_refs. Use verification=verified only when the ledger has positive runtime evidence plus a negative control; use generated-not-verified when S5 can construct concrete replay steps from source/S4 evidence but the ledger lacks full verification; use needs-poc when replay or expected signal is missing. A verified row must have a copyable replay command or a compact ordered step sequence, not a placeholder-only sketch. FINDING% may mirror POC% for legacy GUI compatibility, but POC% is the authoritative output. Finish with a concise PoC bundle, not a narrative report.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s5_adversarial_review",
            stage_id: "stage5",
            stage_code: "S5",
            title: "S5 advisory review lane",
            gate: PhaseGate::S5Review,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "The backend scheduler opens exactly one workflow-owned reviewer thread after the draft PoC bundle. The reviewer must emit advisory REVIEW% comments and one REVIEW_REPORT% line, without rewriting the bundle.",
            body: "Spawn the poc_bundle_review child lane. It reviews only the supplied S5 PoC bundle and runbook ledger for duplicate PoCs, missing replay steps, weak expected signals, unsupported severity, unsafe cleanup/side effects, source-backed entries incorrectly marked verified, and ledger-supported omissions. It must not run tools, inspect source, perform fresh auditing, or rewrite the bundle. Its output is advisory input, not an automatic veto, for the root agent's final correction pass.",
        },
        WorkflowPhase {
            id: "s5_final_revision",
            stage_id: "stage5",
            stage_code: "S5",
            title: "S5 review-driven final revision",
            gate: PhaseGate::S5FinalRevision,
            hard_gate: true,
            contract: "Consume REVIEW_CONTEXT from the advisory review lane, critically apply supported corrections, and emit RUNBOOK% S5 Final Revision plus the corrected canonical POC% set. This phase is output-only: no tool calls and no fresh auditing.",
            body: "Read REVIEW_CONTEXT critically. Reviewer comments are advisory, not authoritative. Accept merge/drop only when the ledger proves same-family duplication, contradiction by controls, out-of-scope scope, placeholder content, or non-security impact. For missing live proof, weak replay, or unclear severity, prefer downgrade/rewrite to generated-not-verified or needs-poc over deletion. Do not let one failed payload variant or one failed chain erase a root family that still supports a concrete replayable PoC. Preserve recall: every credible root family with source/root-cause anchor and plausible exploit impact must remain in the replacement POC% set with an honest verification value. Preserve existing finding= and evidence_refs= whenever the draft row already cites the right ledger items. Quote every long or shell/JSON-bearing POC% field in the replacement rows. If you reject a reviewer suggestion, state the reason briefly. Do not inspect source, run commands, or create new claims beyond REVIEW% action=add_check items that already cite runbook evidence. Emit REVIEW_APPLIED% target=<id-or-family> action=<accepted|rejected> reason=<short> for material comments. Then emit exactly one RUNBOOK% S5 Final Revision line and the corrected canonical POC% rows. Treat this as a replacement PoC set, not an appendix to the draft.",
        },
    ]
}
